*****************************************************************************************
 fiTQun parameter overrides for LUCiD's SK_WAND geometry
*****************************************************************************************

  Tune file lookup. WCSimPMTType names the photosensor and appears in every
  per-PMT file name (cPDFpar_<type>.root, angResp_<config>_<type>.root,
  <pdg>_tpdfpar_<config>_<type>.root); WCSimConfig names the detector and gives
  fiTQun_scattablesF_<config>.root.

     < fiTQun.WCSimPMTType = PMT20inch >
     < fiTQun.WCSimConfig = SK_WAND >

  Scalar parameters. fiTQun builds these names as <key>WCSim, and the shipped
  parameters file defines only the SK<n> variants, so a WCSim tune must supply all
  of them -- step 5 ("tuning the scalar parameters") of the reference procedure,
  which has NOT been done for this detector.

    WaterAttenuationLength. LUCiD's water is the SK calibration parameterisation
      (evaluated as lucid/wavelength/medium.py implements it), and a single
      effective length is convention dependent: 1/<alpha> = 6516 cm, <1/alpha> =
      8705 cm, and fitting one exponential to the spectrum-averaged transmission
      gives 6810 cm at R = 5 m rising to 7718 cm at 40 m as the spectrum hardens.
      Over the source-PMT distances that dominate SK geometry this lands on
      ~7000-7500 cm, so SK-IV's 7496.46 is kept -- it is the fitted value at
      R ~ 30 m. Per-wavelength lengths peak at 121 m near 400 nm.

    QEEff. Cannot be pinned from the QE curve alone: it converts *emitted*
      photons (our gNphot) into detected ones, so it depends on the emission band
      PhotonSim used. The same curve gives <QE> = 16.3% over 300-500 nm, 12.8%
      over the QE knot range 294-648 nm, and 6.7% over 200-700 nm. 0.1278 is the
      knot-range value, which is also LUCiD's own default sampling band. This is
      provisional: simulator.py notes that treating all nphot as detectable
      over-counts charge by ~17% and drives recon dE ~ -13%, and our gNphot runs
      ~14% above the reference tune's. The residual belongs in QEEffCorr, which a
      real scalar tune fits against observed charge; it is left at unity here.

     < fiTQun.PeakThrWCSim = 9. >
     < fiTQun.GainCorrFactWCSim = 1.0 >
     < fiTQun.WaterAttenuationLengthWCSim = 7496.46 >
     < fiTQun.DarkRateWCSim = 4.2 >
     < fiTQun.QEEffWCSim = 0.02278 >
  ^ MEASURED charge scale, not a QE value, obtained by iteration on 20 muons of
  322-2046 MeV/c. The first estimate (0.0319) was fitted while hits were being
  attributed to the wrong PMTs and is void. On correct geometry, QEEff = 0.0319
  gave p_reco/p_true = 0.714 with totq/totmu = 1.008 -- the fit reproduces the
  observed charge but at 0.71x the true momentum, so the light-per-MeV is high by
  that factor: 0.0319 x 0.714 = 0.02278.
  This is step 5 of the reference procedure (tuning the scalar parameters), and
  QEEff is where it has to go for MC: QEEffCorr, the knob that looks made for it,
  is assigned only inside if(IsItRealData()) (fiTQun.cc:685-700) and is forced to
  1 otherwise, so it cannot correct a simulated sample.
  The factor is larger than any plausible mean QE (6.7-16.3% over the curve,
  depending on PhotonSim's emission band), so it is absorbing the whole
  normalisation chain -- Phi0_local, the nphot convention, and the ~14% by which
  our gNphot exceeds the reference tune's -- not QE alone. Re-measure whenever the
  photon yield, QE curve or digitizer changes.
     < fiTQun.QEEffCorrWCSim = 1. >
  p_reco/p_true = 0.2494 (16-84%: 0.204-0.429) over 20 muons of 322-2046 MeV/c,
  i.e. fiTQun predicted 4.01x more light than LUCiD produced, so the fit bought
  agreement by shrinking the momentum. Predicted charge is linear in this factor,
  so scaling it by the measured ratio is the first-order scalar tune (step 5 of
  the reference procedure). It absorbs whatever QEEff cannot be derived for:
  PhotonSim's emission band is unknown, and <QE> over the QE curve ranges from
  6.7% (200-700nm) to 16.3% (300-500nm) depending on it. Re-measure this whenever
  the photon yield, QE curve or digitizer changes.
     < fiTQun.QmuConvFactWCSim = 1.0 >

  PID and ring-counting discriminant cut lines. These affect only the reported
  classification, not the fits themselves; SK-IV's values.

     < fiTQun.PIDCutEPipa0WCSim = -10. >
     < fiTQun.PIDCutEPipa1WCSim = 0. >
     < fiTQun.PIDCutMuPip0WCSim = 20. >
     < fiTQun.PIDCutMuPip1WCSim = 0.2 >
     < fiTQun.RCCut1Ea0WCSim = 87.5 >
     < fiTQun.RCCut1Mua0WCSim = 140. >
     < fiTQun.RCCuta0WCSim = 70. >
     < fiTQun.RCCuta1WCSim = 0. >

  Our Cherenkov profiles are the raw tables (hI3d_*), not the momentum-fitted
  form, so the fitted branch of LoadProfiles must be off or it looks for
  CProf_<pdg>_fit_WCSim.root. The time profile stays fitted: tpdfpar IS the
  fitted parameterisation and that is the file we supply.

  Refractive index the fitter assumes when subtracting time of flight. This is
  not an effective average: LUCiD's refractive_index is a plain scalar 1.33
  (config/materials/water.json -- its scattering and absorption are
  wavelength-dependent, the index is not), so every photon propagates at c/1.33
  and the fitter assuming 1.33 reproduces that exactly, with no residual.
  fiTQun ships 1.38, SK's GROUP index, which is the physically right choice for
  real water but the wrong one for reconstructing this simulation. The tune has
  to match the simulation, not the physics.
  This MUST match the nwtr passed to makehistWCSim when the time PDF is built --
  they are separate constants that merely share a default, so changing one alone
  silently biases the PDF against the fitter.
     < fiTQun.WaterRefractiveIndex = 1.33 >
     < fiTQun.UseFitCProfile = 0 >

  Scattered light from the 6D table only. fiTQun's default UseScatteredLight=4
  means "6D for the 1R fit, 3D otherwise", which demands
  fiTQun_scattable3d_<config>.root -- a separate reference product
  (Utilities/scattable/scattable3d) that this tune does not yet build. 1 selects
  the 6D table everywhere, which is the table we do produce.

     < fiTQun.UseScatteredLight = 1 >

  Which single-ring hypotheses to fit. The shipped defaults enable the proton
  (DoProton1RFit = 2, upstream track), which makes LoadProfiles demand
  CProf_2212_WCSim.root and abort when it is absent -- and no WCSim tune ships a
  proton profile, ours or the reference's (only the SK-era tune does). The
  enabled set must match the profiles the tune actually provides: e, mu, pi+.

     < fiTQun.DoProton1RFit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoKaonPlus1RFit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoGamma1RFit = 0 >

  The pion is fitted as an upstream track (DoPionPlus1RFit = 2), and that fit
  segfaults in SetOneRingSeed/GetOneRngnglogL on this tune -- the upstream-track
  seed depends on the time PDF, which is still a bootstrap from a different
  detector (nuPRISM/WCTE), so its seed lands out of range. Disabled until our own
  time PDF exists; the electron and muon fits run to completion without it.

     < fiTQun.DoPionPlus1RFit = 0 >

  Single-ring fits only, for this demonstration. Everything downstream of the 1R
  fits -- multi-segment, multi-ring, pi0, mu/gamma decay -- builds on the same
  time PDF and multi-ring machinery, and DoMSFit segfaults in GetMRnglogL on the
  bootstrap tune. The quantity being measured here is the 1R muon fit, so the
  rest are off; they should be re-enabled once the time PDF is our own.

     < fiTQun.DoMSFit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoMRFit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoMoreMRFit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoPi0Fit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoPDK_MuGammaFit = 0 >
     < fiTQun.DoInGateDcyeFit = 0 >

  fiTQun ships DoBiasCorrection = 1, which applies EMPIRICAL bias corrections
  derived for Super-K to the fit results. Those corrections are not justified for
  a different detector and silently shift every reconstructed quantity, so they
  are off here. Turn them back on only with corrections derived for this geometry.

     < fiTQun.DoBiasCorrection = 0 >
